{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384484"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"robert louis stevenson and the aesthetics of the adventure game","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384484","ids":["itu-daba3bd6-2cc5-4d7a-8619-e7402b7d149e"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6F2E0CA1AB4BA3142CF3791B3474E0BD"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-daba3bd6-2cc5-4d7a-8619-e7402b7d149e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Robert Louis Stevenson and the aesthetics of the adventure game","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384484","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"With the advent of the retro-gaming movement there is an increasing interest in rediscovering games once played. Tip of my Joystick' is a Reddit community dedicated to the re-finding of forgotten games. In this subreddit, users describe games they wish to re-find so that other users may help them identify the game's title. This community thus offers a unique opportunity for studying how players recall and describe games and play experiences of the past. This paper presents the results of an analysis of a random sample of 250 posts from this subreddit. The posts were analyzed in terms of what aspects of games they describe For the purpose of this analysis we developed a coding scheme consisting of 38 individual codes belonging to 9 different main categories. Our findings may contribute to research on game archiving and collection as they may help inform the design of better game search engines.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","Bogers, Toine"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-065642"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","end":"2020-09-18","place":"Bugibba, mt","start":"2020-09-15","title":"15th International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games, FDG 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Re finding","Video games","complex information needs","game archives","game description","known item search"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"kinda like the sims but with ghosts a qualitative analysis of video game re finding requests on reddit","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-065642","identifier":[{"id":"85092276457","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-35b1c44b-b8ea-4f1e-8f35-3ecffda2db53"],"keyword":["complex information needs","game archives","game description","known-item search","re-finding","video games"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-E2762C749EDD3AAF310416A4C6E3175F"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Science and Information Studies","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Science, Policy and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Toine","key":"bogerspau","last":"Bogers","locid":"c39fa087-a00a-425e-9e0f-7fa301dbe554","name":"Bogers, Toine","orcid":"0000-0003-0716-676X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispedh","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Antonios","key":"liapispedh","last":"Liapis","name":"Liapis, Antonios","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kyburz","key":"pennypedh","last":"Penny","name":"Penny, Kyburz","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vanessa","key":"volzpedh","last":"Volz","name":"Volz, Vanessa","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Foaad","key":"khosmoodpedh","last":"Khosmood","name":"Khosmood, Foaad","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Phil","key":"lopespedh","last":"Lopes","name":"Lopes, Phil","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3402942.3402971","eisbn":["9781450388078"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)","series":"ACM International Conference Proceeding Series","status":"p","title":"FDG '20: Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85092276457"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-35b1c44b-b8ea-4f1e-8f35-3ecffda2db53"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"\"Kinda like the Sims... but with ghosts?\": A Qualitative Analysis of Video Game Re-finding Requests on Reddit","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-065642","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"With the advent of the retro-gaming movement there is an in-creasing interest in rediscovering games once played. ‘Tip of my Joystick’ is a Reddit community dedicated to the re-finding of for-gotten games. In this subreddit, users describe games they wish to re-find so that other users may help them identify the game’s title. This community thus offers a unique opportunity for studying how players recall and describe games and play experiences of the past. This paper presents the results of an analysis of a random sample of 250 posts from this subreddit. The posts were analyzed in terms of what aspects of games they describe. For the purpose of this analysis we developed a coding scheme consisting of 38 individual codes belonging to 9 different main categories. Our findings may contribute to research on game archiving and collection as they may help inform the design of better game search engines.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","Bogers, Toine"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384862"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","title":"Foundations of Digital Games"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Re finding","Video games","complex information needs","game archives","game description","known item search"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"kinda like the sims but with ghosts a qualitative analysis of video game re finding requests on reddit","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85386142/FDG_20_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384862","identifier":[{"id":"85092276457","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f45b62bb-4eae-413f-9c98-3f3fab36907a"],"keyword":["complex information needs","game archives","game description","known-item search","re-finding","video games"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-E2762C749EDD3AAF310416A4C6E3175F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85386142/FDG_20_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Toine","key":"bogerspau","last":"Bogers","name":"Bogers, Toine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispedh","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Antonios","key":"liapispedh","last":"Liapis","name":"Liapis, Antonios","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Penny","key":"kyburzpedh","last":"Kyburz","name":"Kyburz, Penny","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3402942.3402971","eisbn":["9781450388078"],"pages":"1-4","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"FDG '20: Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85092276457"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f45b62bb-4eae-413f-9c98-3f3fab36907a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"\"Kinda like The Sims... But with ghosts?\" : A Qualitative Analysis of Video Game Re-finding Requests on Reddit","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384862","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Computer games and board games alike are cultural artifacts that reflect the ideas of their creators and the society in which they are embedded and played. Like cinema and literature, they may represent more or less familiar environments, events and beings. That said, there are great differences between these media, which also affects how they can be understood as representational artifacts. In addition to this, when considering a game such as Tetris, it is not even given that games represent in the first place. This raises the question of what it is that makes us consider games as representational artifacts and subsequently, which representational capacities, or better, meaning-producing capacities and practices we may associate with them? Furthermore, to what extend are games a medium and how do they relate to other media? This dissertation reviews theories of representation and their application to game analysis. Based on these theories, I propose a media-centered, and materialist framework for conceptualizing and analyzing games as simulations. With this, I will address games as objects, consisting of a multimodal surface expression that is governed by an underlying mechanical structure and realized in a material medium. The framework describes the basic qualities of a given game through what I call the material, structural, communicational and agential modalities. I will discuss the ways in which simulations refer to a target, a relationship which I argue is not intrinsic to the simulation itself, but remains relative to interpretation. Consequently, the study of representation should not only consider the game artifact in itself, but also the context in which it is produced and consumed. Therefore, the analysis will progress from the relatively formal analysis of games as simulations to a discussion of games as cultural artifacts that are discursively constructed as what I call qualified media. These qualified media have evolved side by side with other media, and are governed by a set of normative assumptions about what games are, what they should look like, how they should be played and by whom. Finally, the dissertation will offer a demonstration of the proposed framework in an analysis of the ways in which gender is represented in the computer game The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt. This analysis will first consider the formal elements of the game, that is the material, structural, visual and textual aspects and how they are put into operation by a player. Next, the case study situates the game and its construction of gender in a broader cultural context in which games intersect with other media and are produced and consumed according to certain cultural conventions and norms.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381323"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Games","Gender","MODELS","Media","Media history","Representation","Simulation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"games as representational artifacts a media centered analytical approach to representation in games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067155/PhD_Thesis_Final_Version_Ida_Katrine_Hammeleff_J_rgensen.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381323","ids":["itu-046bb469-1582-404b-8f3c-62de5836d3f5"],"keyword":["Representation","games","gender","media","media history","models","simulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B83FBDE785D2DF6B733EDAAC3BD8210B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067155/PhD_Thesis_Final_Version_Ida_Katrine_Hammeleff_J_rgensen.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779490369"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"168","pages":"342","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-046bb469-1582-404b-8f3c-62de5836d3f5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Games as representational artifacts : A media-centered analytical approach to representation in games","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381323","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"This paper offers a discussion of the ways in which games interface with a variety of different media - in the broadest sense. The starting point of this discussion is the uneasy relationship between games and the concept of media, which has been treated in different ways in game studies. Some scholars argue that computer games make up a distinct medium with an expressive power that sets them of from other media (Bogost 2010). Other scholars seem to think of games as a form of ‘content’ that may be mediated by different technical media, such as the computer (Eskelinen 2001; Juul 2010). Finally, Aarseth (2004) argues, that in cybertext, the medium no longer simply transmits, but rather generates content, which make it questionable if it even makes sense to consider this an instance of mediation at all. However, the concept of medium also comes with its own set of problems and highly different theoretical approaches. As such, it has been used to point to as different phenomena as modes of communication, technical channels, sets of expressive conventions or instructions, social practices or particular institutions or industries (Mitchell and Hansen 2010)In regards to games, this then leaves us with a number of questions such as if all or only some games essentially constitute a medium, and if the latter is so, which games may then be considered media and what sets them off from the rest, and finally in accordance with what sense(s) of the concept of medium. This paper however, aims to bypass the question of whether games are essentially a medium or not, in favor of an approach which considers in a historical perspective, how games have been interfacing with other phenomena that has been associated with the concept of medium, but also how this affects the communicational conventions of games today. Ellestrom’s (2010) notion of qualified media will frame this discussion. Elleström (2017) considers qualified media as ‘abstract’ or even ‘ideal’ conceptualizations of particular groups of media products (which are in themselves multi-modal). In order to analyze what makes up these qualified media, Elleström suggests two aspects, namely the context in which a qualified medium emerged, and the aesthetic and communicative norms that is considered distinct for this qualified medium. Similarly, this paper will explore the context in which games began to be associated with the media concept on one hand and on the other the contemporary ideas we may have about the expressive power of the so-called ‘medium/media of games.’ These two aspects, it will be argued, are closely linked, as the contemporary assumptions about the representational capacity of games, are in part a result of the ways in which games have been positioned vis a vis other forms of media. This paper will focus on two historical moments, first, the industrialization of the nineteenth century, which brought with them not only so-called proprietary games, but also the emergence of the concept of communications media (Guillory 2010)), and second the appropriation of computer technology in the leisure industry in the second half of the 20th century. The paper will follow the influence of different gaming practices, such as public, domestic, hobby, and casual gaming, the influence of other qualified media, such as literature and cinema, and technical artifacts such as the computer, and the screen.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384043"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Board Games","Cross media","Digital Games","Games","Intermediality","Media","Media history"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the qualifications of games as media","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384043","ids":["itu-bdb2a5b0-aa3e-4c81-903b-c2ff782e647f"],"keyword":["Intermediality","board games","cross-media","digital games","games","media","media history"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3683E3AF6154A7CAB3D0BE1F6727E83B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-bdb2a5b0-aa3e-4c81-903b-c2ff782e647f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The qualifications of games as media","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384043","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This paper addresses the relationship between games and media and discusses to which extend games can be conceived as media, and how they should be studied as such. The paper proposes a revised version of Elleström’s intermedial framework that has been harnessed to better describe games. The framework acknowledges the heterogeneous nature of not only games but all media. Elleström’s framework allows us to address games as particular tangible objects in the world in a way that avoids normative assumptions about games in general as well as specific types of games such as digital or analog games. This framework has several usages. It can be applied in an analysis the relationship between different modalities in a single game; it can be used to compare different games; and finally, it can be used to discuss the similarities and differences between games and other media objects.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383531"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","title":"Foundations of Digital Games"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Comparison","Games","Intermediality","MODES","Media","Mediation","Modalities","cybertext"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"media and games an intermedial framework","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83586834/Media_and_games_post_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383531","identifier":[{"id":"85055499614","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9b05e2a2-e7e5-4052-ae35-46917ea20ece"],"keyword":["Intermediality","Mediation","comparison","cybertext","games","media","modalities","modes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B3D15D13F89BB17FFD8D35CAD4C947B3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83586834/Media_and_games_post_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Steve","key":"dahlskogpedh","last":"Dahlskog","name":"Dahlskog, Steve","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Sebastian","key":"deterdingpedh","last":"Deterding","name":"Deterding, Sebastian","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"José","key":"fontpedh","last":"Font","name":"Font, José","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mitu","key":"khandakerpedh","last":"Khandaker","name":"Khandaker, Mitu","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Carl Magnus","key":"olssonpedh","last":"Olsson","name":"Olsson, Carl Magnus","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Sebastian","key":"risipedh","last":"Risi","name":"Risi, Sebastian","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Christoph","key":"salgepedh","last":"Salge","name":"Salge, Christoph","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3235765.3235794","eisbn":["978 14503 6571 0"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"Foundations of Digital Games Conference Proceedings","status":"p","subtitle":"August 7–10, 2018, Malmö, Sweden","title":"FDG '18 Proceedings of the 13th International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85055499614"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9b05e2a2-e7e5-4052-ae35-46917ea20ece"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Media and games: an intermedial framework","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383531","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"In the long history of board games, certain games have been passed on in generations and have evolved and been shaped to fit different cultural and religious contexts and functions (Parlett 1999). More recently we have seen popular board games being repackaged to fit pop-cultural themes retrieved from television or cinema (Woods 2012). Furthermore, since the early days of digital games, tabletop games have served as a source of inspiration for many video-game designers, and more recently we have seen the occurrence of tabletop game adaptations of popular video-games such as StarCraft (Blizzard 2010). Such adaptations make use of different strategies for capturing the various aspects of their source. Talisman: Digital Edition (Nomad Games 2014) largely adopts the same primary mechanics of the board game Talisman (Fantasy Flight Games 2008) while StarCraft: The Board Game (Fantasy Flight Games 2007) adopts characters and background story from its source but uses a completely different mechanical system. As Wittgenstein (1958) pointed out, the concept of games however, refers to a range of very different and not easily comparable phenomena. Furthermore, the examples above travels across media with very different spatial and temporal logic (Backe 2015). Applying the concept of adaptations to such games is therefore not straightforward raises the question of what exactly is this an adaptation of? Within the field of adaptation studies, adaptations are typically understood as exchanges between literature and cinema (Elleström 2013). However, recent adaptation theories such as Hutcheon (2006) stresses that adaptations should not only be limited to the so-called narrative media. Instead Hutcheon defines adaptations as repetition with variation (Ibid). Hutcheon’s definition makes it possible to study similarities and differences equally and thus challenges the ‘ideology of fidelity’ that has long permeated the field of adaption studies. This presentation explores adaptations between digital and non-digital games. This analysis is inspired by intermedial studies (e.g. Elleström 2010) that distinguishes between different modalities of media. Relying on Aarseth’s and Calleja’s (2015) model of the cybermedia object, I will thus present three different aspect of games that can be adapted from digital to non-digital and vice-versa: 1) the material artifact, 2) the game mechanics and 3) the representational layer. This categorization will be discussed through a number of different case-studies of game adaptations between digital and non-digital games. In these case-studies the adaptation will be compared to its source in terms of the three different aspects and the adaptations will furthermore be compared to each other. Suggested literature: Aarseth, E and Calleja, G. 2015. ”The Word Game: The Ontology of an Indefinable Object” in Proceedings of the 10th International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games (FDG 2015), June 22-25, 2015, Pacific Grove, CA, USA. Aarseth, E. 2011. “’Define Real, Moron!’ Some Remarks on Game Ontologies.” DIGAREC Keynote- Lectures 2009/10. Edited by Stephan Günzel, Michael Liebe and Dieter Mersch: 50-69. Potsdam: University Press. Aarseth, E. 1997. Cybertext. Perspective on Ergodic litterature. Baltimore: The Johns Hopkins University Press. Backe, H-J. 2015. “Where’s BattleTech in MechWarrior Online? A Case Study in Game Adaptation” Well Played 4(1): 11-34. Elleström, L. 2013. “Adaptation within the field of media transformations. In: Adaptation Studies. New Challenges, New Directions. Edited by Jørgen Bruhn, Anne Gjelsvik and Eirik Frisvold Hanssen. London: Bloomsbury. Elleström, L. 2010. “The Modalities of Media: A Model for Understanding Intermedial Relations” In: Media Borders, Multimodality and Intermediality. Edited by Lars Elleström: 11- 50. Basingstoke: Palgrave Macmillan UK. Elliot, K. 2013. “Theorizing adaptations/adapting theories” In: Adaptation Studies. New Challenges, New Directions. Edited by Jørgen Bruhn, Anne Gjelsvik and Eirik Frisvold Hanssen. London: Bloomsbury. Hutcheon, L. 2006. A Theory of Adaptation. New York: Routledge Parlett, D. 1999. The Oxford History of Board Games. Oxford: Oxford University Press. Wittgenstein, L. 1958. Philosophical Investigations, second edition. Oxford: Basil Blackwell. Woods, S. 2012. Eurogames: The Design, Culture and Play of Modern European board games. Jefferson, NC: McFarland & Company, Inc., Publishers Suggested ludography: Blizzard Entertainment. 1998. StarCraft. Exozet Games GmBh. Carcassonne 2017. Fantasy Flight Games. 2004. Doom: The Board game. Fantasy Flight Games. 2007. StarCraft: The Board game. Games Workshop. 2007. Talisman: The magical quest Game. id Software. 1993. Doom. Irrational Games. 2013. BioShock: Infinite. Klaus-Jürgen Wrede/ Rio Grande Games. 2000. Carcassonne. Nomad Games. 2014. Talisman. Digital Edition. Plaid Hat Games. 2013. BioShock Infinite: The Siege of Columbia.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381540"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adaptations","Board Games","Cross media","Digital Games","Games","Intermediality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"adaptations in play strategies of adaptation in digital and non digital games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://boardgamestudies.files.wordpress.com/2017/06/jc3b8rgensen-abstract-boardgame-workshop-adaptations.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381540","ids":["itu-1253dffc-c46e-4093-89ed-bc955af36e83"],"keyword":["Adaptations","Board Games","Cross-media","Digital Games","Games","Intermediality"],"l