","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3e60c75f-7836-4ee4-af04-bcb4e10d799c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Zombies, aliens, monsters, and slime : Making sense of the mess through a taxonomy of non-human representations in games","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382190","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Recently self-referentiality have occurred as a trend among game designers and have also enjoyed sporadic attention in academia. However, in academia, discussions of self-referential games often rest on proceduralist arguments and a too exclusive focus on the game object. This paper draws on the typology of meta-pictures developed by art historian J.W.T. Mitchell. Based on this typology, this paper discusses the notion of meta-games and suggest a broad conception of such games that includes not only the game object, but also the player and the discourse in which it is interpreted.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384805"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Games","Meta games","Referentiality","Representation","self reflexive games"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a game is a game is a game","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://digra2017.com/static/Extended%20Abstracts/55_DIGRA2017_EA_Jorgensen_Game_Is_Game.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384805","ids":["itu-f0251c5d-3830-48c7-9bf7-e1c60a99efcc"],"keyword":["Games","Meta-games","Referentiality","Representation","self-reflexive games"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-422EA5972B839AFDECD02B56B4B6CCEA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://digra2017.com/static/Extended%20Abstracts/55_DIGRA2017_EA_Jorgensen_Game_Is_Game.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f0251c5d-3830-48c7-9bf7-e1c60a99efcc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A game is a game is a game","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384805","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","Blom, Johanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381963"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"polyphonic representation in game narratives","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82349068/Gameslit_abstract.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381963","ids":["itu-2f1aa66a-fff5-4cfb-95fb-baf26da292c0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-85D40DE9F215DD0798AE64CE102C3725"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82349068/Gameslit_abstract.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Johanna","key":"blompau","last":"Blom","name":"Blom, Johanna","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2f1aa66a-fff5-4cfb-95fb-baf26da292c0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Polyphonic Representation in Game Narratives","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381963","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"This article discusses methodological considerations of user-centred design for non-human animals. These considerations are illustrated through a design research project that aims to apply digital technology to build games for orangutans’ enrichment. The article argues that design for other species reveals limitations of designers’ knowledge of prospected users. The article explores how to approach participants who cannot express themselves verbally and how to recognise play that may not look familiar to the designer. The article finally presents a participatory design method that allows for non-human contributions in design. This method applies play as an interspecies co-creative act and can be used as a starting point for addressing questions of difference in play and designing games that allow for ambiguous play.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","Wirman, Hanna Elina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383213"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["game design non human animal play non human animal creativity design research participation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multispecies methods technologies for play","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82437717/multispecies_methods_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383213","identifier":[{"id":"84961205558","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-841fae48-4a90-48af-a422-78a895c5110b"],"keyword":["game design; non-human animal play; non-human animal creativity; design research; participation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-05D552FB1B5A9C7A31584127C9F92266"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82437717/multispecies_methods_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","The Department, Culture and Games"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanna Elina","key":"wirmanpau","last":"Wirman","locid":"9ba013db-4c4d-41dd-be12-dbe188f67f29","name":"Wirman, Hanna Elina","orcid":"0000-0002-9578-2708","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/14626268.2016.1144617","issn":["14626268","17443806"],"issn_org":["14626268"],"issue":"1","journal":"Digital Creativity","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Digital Creativity","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84961205558"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-841fae48-4a90-48af-a422-78a895c5110b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multispecies methods, technologies for play","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383213","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Instinctive stands in opposition to thoughtful. It is free from causal reasoning and deductive logic that bases on knowledge from past experience (Garrett 1997). Acknowledging how little is confirmed about the origins, importance or evolution of play in humans and non-humans (e.g. Burghardt 2005), the part of instinctual also remains a mystery. In Wirman’s recent studies (e.g. Wirman 2015), however, we can recognise a human tendency to see ‘instinctive’ in animal behaviour while ‘play’ substitutes it in categorising human actions. This poses intriguing questions for multispecies game and play studies: Why is it commonsensical to label an animal’s touch screen interaction as ‘instinctive’? While we consider a human ‘playing’ a game? How much do we actually know about the instinctive aspects of human and non-human animal play behaviour? What is the specific importance and role of instincts in human play? What kind of definition of ‘instinctual’ is meaningful for the study of games and play? Finally, how to design for instinctual or for non-instinctual play? While many such questions remain out of the reach of a humanist or a design researcher, this paper aims to focus on one single aspect of instinctive play. The paper looks at different types of games from physical duels to online multiplayers and based on those identify ‘instances of instinctual’ in play. The paper presumes that game mechanics and game platforms frame and limit player’s possibilities for purely non-deductive approach and it is very much the nature of games to force their player to learn to continue. Learning in itself, as a foundation for achievement, is based on reasoning and seemingly opposite to instinct. From this point of view, extended play (Myers 2010) and progression are keys to understanding and separating instinctive from non-instinctive in gameplay. Fortunately, the field of primate studies and animal play lend themselves well to the study of instinctive in play. Side by side, the paper studies how such research approaches instinctive animal play behaviour and what is said about motivations for different types of play in the broadly understood field of games studies. Claiming play itself as an instinct (e.g. Caillois 1958/2001; Gray 2013) is beyond the question under consideration. The paper draws from Huizinga (1950/2014) where he writes that reasons to play form a phenomenon far more complex than a single (innate) instinct. Caillois’ (2001) game types are nevertheless considered in parallel to ‘instinctive.’ When Caillois describes his concept of free form play, paidia, he calls these “manifestations of the play instinct.” This brings the paper back to the question of different species’ play tendencies as Caillois uses examples of a cat, a dog and an infant those describe those who engage in paidia kind of play. Here a specific type of play is associated with instincts and typically assumes a ‘lower’ skill or capacity of maneuvering an object. This paper will continue this line of thought further investigating what is it that constitutes instinctive in multispecies game studies of different game types and playing styles. References Burghardt, G.M. The genesis of animal play: Testing the limits. Cambridge, MA, The MIT Press, 2005. Caillois, R. Man, Play and Games. Urbana and Chicago, University of Illinois Press, 2001. Gray, P. Free to Learn: Why Unleashing the Instinct to Play Will Make Our Children Happier, More Self-Reliant, and Better Students for Life. New York, Basic Books, 2013. Garrett, D. Cognition and Commitment in Hume’s Philosophy. Oxford University Press. New York, New York, 1997. Huizinga, J. Homo Ludens: A Study of the Play-Element in Culture. Ct, USA, Martino Publishing, 1950/2014. Myers, D. Play redux: The form of computer games. The University of Michigan Press and The University of Michigan Library, Ann Arbor, 2010. Wirman, H. Uncomfortable Resemblance in Online Animal-technology Videos. Minding Animals Conference, New Delhi, India, 13-20 January 2015.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Wirman, Hanna Elina","Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382524"],"event":{"end":"2015-10-24","place":"Krakow, pl","start":"2015-10-21","title":"Central and Eastern European Game Studies Conference 2015","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Animal play","Game genres","Instinct","Multispecies research","Playing styles"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"instinct ive play behaviour in human and non human players","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-382524","ids":["itu-53e5aad7-3d3f-40f7-a03e-a27c980a2088"],"keyword":["Animal play","Game genres","Instinct","Multispecies research","Playing styles"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-99F9A8FF9A33B7E213F1D1B7E2AFD493"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanna Elina","key":"wirmanpau","last":"Wirman","locid":"9ba013db-4c4d-41dd-be12-dbe188f67f29","name":"Wirman, Hanna Elina","orcid":"0000-0002-9578-2708","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Ida Kathrine Hammeleff","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-53e5aad7-3d3f-40f7-a03e-a27c980a2088"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Instinct(ive) play behaviour in human and non-human players","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382524","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
